WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Swords and Serpents, step into a new world of gaming, where you not only play, you become.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:29.660
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.380
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.180
And I'm also Joe. Is Swords and Serpents a play on Snakes and Ladders?

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.600
I don't think so.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.560
Yeah, not in the gameplay anywhere.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.480
Snakes and Ladders is just like a...

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.580
Snakes and Ladders?

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.840
Yeah. Oh, it's like Shoots and Ladders, right?

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.160
Oh, we gotta make it cool for kids in the 90s.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.560
I'd say it's just Dungeons and Dragons by another name.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.160
Yeah, that's actually a really good point.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.380
My whole argument is thrown out the window because I only ever played Snakes and Ladders.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:04.000
I am aware of Shoots and Ladders, though, which this is obviously not doing that.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.280
So, forget about that.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:07.940
Serpents with an S, though?

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.360
A little misleading, right?

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.500
There's just like one serpent at the end.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.440
No. I ran into a couple of serpents and I didn't get anywhere close to the end.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.960
Okay. I thought there was like one big dragon serpent thing at the end that like that's what they

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.560
were referring to. I don't remember any serpents.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.040
I thought that they were all just like dudes in armor or like goblins or stuff like that.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.920
Yeah, this... I didn't get very far, though.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.120
But this dungeon is crowded, Sean, with like everybody.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.100
It's like, are all these people thematically working together?

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.200
Like, are they all part of the same army trying to take us down?

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.420
Because like there's no rhyme or reason to who you run into in this game.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.040
I don't remember any like actual deep lore.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.520
I think it's just sort of you get thrown in there, you fight stuff, and then you beat

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.460
a dude. And is there deep lore?

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.420
I didn't read the manual.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.520
I guess I've just meant like the types of enemies.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:03.940
It's like, okay, I can believe that the spiders and the skeletons all like live inside the

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:07.520
dungeon, right? But there's a lot of like other types of warriors and...

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.300
It's like a mailman.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.540
Yeah, there's no mailman.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.920
But like different types of like warriors that you'll run into and goblins and like I

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.000
just did none of them feel like they're all like working together.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.020
And who would they be working together for anyway?

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.140
The big dragon at the end, like he commands this army?

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.980
Yeah, I mean as someone who also didn't read any like lore, if it is in the manual, I read

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.380
a lot of the manual, but none of the like story stuff.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:40.080
But I just assumed that like all of the soldiers were part of like one army and I don't know

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.960
why they were in there, but like they wanted to stop us from getting to the end.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:50.440
And then everything else that's not a like soldier or like I guess a wizard or something.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.860
Everything that's not a human is just like the monsters that live in the dungeon.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.540
But that's just that's just my headcanon.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.160
Yeah, I mean there's really not much that there's really not much complexity going on.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:06.360
These are all standard genre fare and you would find them in any fantasy dungeon.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.560
Yeah, but I don't know if you'd find them all like together.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.340
Like, you know, you'd be like, oh, we're gonna go go to the gnome village, meet the gnomes,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.080
right? And then take on like in the local cave, whoever like has been terrorizing the gnomes, right?

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.620
Instead, it's like it's all just happening in this dungeon.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.040
Everything is here. Even the shops are inside the dungeon.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:25.980
It's like Smash Bros.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.480
Yeah, everybody's here.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.200
There's actually no outside world.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.320
They all live in this underground cave.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:32.800
There is no above ground.

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:33.780
We wouldn't know.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.280
We wouldn't know because we don't leave it.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.880
But let's rewind for a minute.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.580
Swords and Serpents is a first person dungeon crawling RPG.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.200
We've played a lot of those.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.160
So we've got to figure out something to talk about that's going to make this one different.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.440
And believe it or not, there is something that's different.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:56.800
Yes, the core loop involves exploring this grid dungeon, turn, you know, turn based combat,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.580
random enemy encounters, you know, defeating enemies, collecting loot, gaining experience.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:10.680
But for the first time ever in a first person NES dungeon crawler, there is a like a map on the screen.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:25.720
Very, very few games that I can think of are not only do they not do this, like this is the first time I'm seeing one in an NES game, but also like it's generating the map as you're moving around it, too.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:27.920
So you don't just like see the whole map.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.800
You didn't like find the map in the dungeon.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.920
And then all of a sudden the map is filled out in your UI.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:37.240
You are filling it in as you're taking steps.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.780
You have this arrow that shows you which direction you're facing in the dungeon.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:46.220
It draws walls for you so you can see the boundaries of where you are.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:54.440
But there's still a black void everywhere else that you have to kind of, you know, explore and fill in to find out everything.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:56.400
Yeah, you got that fog of war.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:08.920
And it's funny that like only a few weeks ago we were talking about wizardry and talking about like all these sort of these functions that didn't really age well.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.520
And we were like, oh, you know, if only they just had like a map on the screen and like, oh, you know, there's a game that just came out in early 2024.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.780
That's a remake of this wizardry.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.560
It's like, oh, wow, they really modernize it.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:32.880
And lo and fucking behold, literally, however, like maybe a month later in the calendar of NES releases, this game is out and it's already got the map.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:34.800
And just come on, wizardry.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:36.320
Now you're making us look bad.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:38.220
Well, I mean, I didn't play wizardry yet.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.540
Well, I will get to it.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:49.260
But compared to any other game, not even just like dungeon crawler games, any other game that had a mode like this where you're like in first person going through a dungeon.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:58.740
This map has made quality of life so much easier and it has made it like intuitive and it made it feel like a game to me rather than whatever.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:04.040
Like, I don't know, Golgo 13 sections like this feel like or other games like that where I'm like, oh, this is cute.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.620
But like, I'm completely lost and this is not fun.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:10.320
This like this made a huge difference having this map on the side.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:24.420
And if you guys peruse the manual at all, you'll notice that a lot of just the first person dungeon crawling experience, while it may be simplified compared to other entries we've experienced that tried to go more complex.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:41.360
This game really feels like it's trying to be the entry level into not just a dungeon crawler, but RPGs in general going as far as to have sections on what's a role playing game and explaining like how each screen on the UI works.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:44.560
And I'm not talking about like how every manual does like most manuals.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.500
Yeah, they'll say like, and this is your map screen, right?

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:53.580
But this actually explains like the difference between your 3D viewing section and your map display.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:07:15.360
So for those of you that haven't played the game, the UI is basically so that there is the 3D first person view in the top left and then in the top right is a top down map that is being drawn as you move through the dungeon, kind of filling out where the walls are, where the doors are, where you've been.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.600
And then you can kind of try to figure out where to go next as a result.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.660
And then at the bottom, you have very basic information about your party.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:24.740
You got a bar chart.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.200
Yeah, you get a bar.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:27.240
Yeah, it really is.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.700
It's a bar graph chart of just like your life.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:35.280
And if anybody can cast spells, just how much MP they have.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:43.520
And while I do think that this game is a little more basic than other RPGs that we've played at this point,

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:51.680
it is really just a lot of walking through a dungeon, running into random encounters, and attacking enemies until you defeat them.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:08:03.620
I will say that just the way that they took the extra time here to like think about how to do a first person game in 8-bit really paid off because it is, to Joe's point,

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.700
like it is so much easier to understand where you are.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:13.080
And not just because there's a map, but because you have two different ways of looking at everything.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.120
It's not like you disregard the 3D screen.

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:20.680
You just have this other way of being like, well, which direction was I coming from?

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.240
Oh, yeah, which direction am I facing?

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:26.260
Like the top-down map really helps establish.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:31.800
And I think a part of that is, while I didn't read the manual, I did do a little bit of background.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.180
This is like a game.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:35.420
This isn't a port.

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:39.040
This is a game that was designed to be played on the NES.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:56.740
So it doesn't have all those like sort of antique features that like wizardry had or like, you know, basically you're running around this dungeon and you don't have to delve through too many menus.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:03.280
There's still menu diving, but it's not quite as antiquated as before.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.780
And also like this is Interplay and their bread and butter has been this kind of game.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:17.460
I just wasn't, I wasn't expecting like, you know, these are the guys behind like Wasteland and Bard's Tale, like them to take on this like,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:23.060
all right, let's do a streamlined 8-bit console version of what we kind of know best.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:24.960
So it's really interesting.

00:09:25.420 --> 00:09:32.560
Yeah, and when you say streamlined too, like they, it's both, I think it's great for like someone who's not super into these games.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.420
I mean, even like attack is A.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.580
You just press A and your character attacks and that's it.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:41.020
There's no, there's not like a big menu or anything.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.580
You press B and you get your menu of spells and that's it.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:52.600
Like, so you can, you can get through a battle quite quickly compared to a lot of other RPGs we've played on the NES where everything is a menu.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:53.840
You have to select a bunch of things.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.440
It kind of, it's almost like a no brainer now that I think about it.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:04.460
Like why haven't other, you know, like, you know, why can't other games, maybe not Final Fantasy, but other games just be like A, attacks.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:08.260
I mean, some might also say that this is like overly simplified.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.640
So we haven't gotten to the qualitative moment of it.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:18.720
Sure, sure. But I do, I do think that like having a button for the thing you're going to do the most is kind of a cool idea.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.540
And then another button that opens a menu of the other options.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:29.920
Yeah. So let's, let's start with that turn-based combat since that's mostly what you're going to be doing as you walk through this dungeon maze, whatever you want to call it.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:39.040
The battles that, you know, you always run into, you can run into multiple of an enemy, but you can't run into groups of various enemies.

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:39.860
Is that right?

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.520
It never happened to me.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:42.620
My understanding was that.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:43.740
I think they're all the same enemy.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:51.940
Yeah. Like, so if you ran into a spider, you could face seven spiders, but you're just going to like, you're, you're only facing just spiders.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:57.000
And so there's no like, Oh, I have to manage like the wizard and his six spiders.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.800
Inside the turn-based combat, they, you know, Joe explained that basically you can either attack or you can use the spells there.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.120
There, you do learn spells as time goes on in the game.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:15.060
You start off with a few if you have like the, the wizard class, but, or no, it's magicians, right?

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.320
They're not wizards. Magicians and clerics.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:21.040
Magicians and thieves and warriors.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.620
There's, I don't think there's clerics.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.200
Right. Sorry.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.780
Uh, but I, I guess you, well, yeah, cause they all learn the spell, right?

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:30.740
Like, so the spells are on the walls in the, in the game.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:35.700
And so basically that's how you learn the spell, but then everybody learns it who can.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:37.020
Correct. Yeah.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.660
Which is like, why can't that be applied to the other things that happen in this game?

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.900
Like, okay, you defeat the enemy and whoever's first in your party receives the broadsword.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:51.380
But wait a minute, that character can't use the broadsword.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:12:02.240
So you have to go into, uh, even though there was a lack of menus in the turn-based combat, there's plenty of menu navigation when you're trying to do anything with your characters.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:07.280
Because you have to enter, um, uh, there, there's, there's three different menus.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:08.680
There's B for your spells.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.640
There's start for like your systems.

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:14.120
And then there's select for like your inventory and other things.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:19.840
And even when you press select for your inventories, it asks you if you want to view one character or the group.

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.740
And viewing the group is kind of a useless thing.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:24.240
It's not like it makes it to this way.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:25.900
You can see what everybody has equipped.

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:27.360
For that, you have to go individual.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:42.940
Individual. And then in the individual equipment, you have to now like select a character who received the broadsword in the fight, trade the equipment over to a character that you hope is capable of having the broadsword equipped, go into that character, and then equip them with it.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:47.860
It is, I just feel like we've seen easier ways to get this done already.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:57.120
And it is a game that is supposed to be, at least my understanding, supposed to be a little like simpler and easier to digest for those.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:03.360
Yeah, sure. What's funny is like, so, so it's very unevenly applied, this whole like streamlining thing.

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.020
They cut down classes down to three.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.400
There's three stats.

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:17.840
Um, uh, but then you've got these other weird like holdovers from, uh, like the menu diving of, of the older generation, I guess.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:24.500
But I also think this might be a side effect of the other kind of feature that we haven't talked about yet.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:26.800
And that's the fact that you can play this game multiplayer.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:35.060
So it would kind of make sense to say like, oh, uh, this is my inventory, not your inventory.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:38.940
Even though, uh, all I can do is press A.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:48.760
Like if you were playing this multiplayer with friends and you had a warrior, like all they can do is press A to attack, which is very funny.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:59.000
But I also think that that might be why each individual character has an individual inventory that they would have to voluntarily trade to you.

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:02.200
They can also choose to flee the fight and say it's up to you.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:06.840
True. I think that's like, I did, I did think about that.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:08.540
Sorry, Joe. I just wanted to.

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:10.300
Go ahead. No, go ahead. While we're on multiplayer.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:19.140
Uh, just, I do wonder, like, is it, is it just a cool feature to have the ability to play with, uh, you know, either two or four?

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:20.920
Sorry, there's no three player multiplayer.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.580
Uh, is it just a cool feature to have?

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:36.360
Or does the game actually work in this, like, not competitive, but like friendly way of being a party with like different types of characters, all like having their own strengths and weaknesses?

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:43.560
Like, I don't think this game necessarily like did enough to lend itself to like, this is an RPG party game.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.120
Like instead, it's just kind of.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.560
I don't think so. Yeah.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:49.740
To Sean's point, it's like some characters just only attack.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:51.360
Like that's the thing they do.

00:14:51.540 --> 00:15:00.580
And they don't get to like, oh, well I can, because I'm, uh, the thief, like I can go talk to this person and have a different dialogue conversation than the warrior would.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.280
So this is now my side quest.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.060
You know, it's like, that's not happening.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:11.140
Yeah, you basically, the only difference here is that the player one is going to still be like the main character.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:19.080
They're the ones that get to walk around and the other characters, you're basically just outsourcing battle control of one of the characters.

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:20.620
Yeah. I mean, that's true.

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.140
And, and I, I like, I both agree and disagree.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:29.240
It's definitely, yeah, you are, if you're playing as a warrior and in multiplayer, yes, you're just pressing A.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:15:59.220
Okay.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:03.160
I'm telling you that like each character does have agency.

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:06.180
It seems. Um, I did play multiplayer.

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:11.000
I played also single player and, uh, I, you know, I didn't get super far either way.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.640
I think it got to the third level in multiplayer and I got to the fifth level in single player.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.640
I did have a lot more fun playing multiplayer.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:22.160
Like, and it did, we, we did feel like we were working together and like deciding who's going to do what.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.620
And like, yeah, we could, you could play single player and everyone just talk and decide what to do.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:35.820
But there, there is something like imaginative, but also like there's, there's something about having this attachment to your character and building up your stats.

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:41.340
Granted, everybody levels up at the same time, but you are like making trades for like, oh, you got a broad sword and like I can use it.

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:42.560
And then this other character can use it.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:43.780
Like who can use the broad sword?

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:49.840
Like, you know, I'll take this and, uh, you know, can you heal me so I can keep attacking or I'm going to flee because my health low and things like that.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:54.560
It, it's, it does feel a little bit like it's a D and D party.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:56.280
You know what this reminded me of?

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:57.260
Basic version of this.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:08.320
